div></div><div class="a-profile-content"><span class="a-profile-name">RodeHaus</span></div></div></div><div class="_Y3Itd_single-review-star-rating-bar-mobile_1VEFO"><i data-hook="review-star-rating" class="a-icon a-icon-star-small a-star-small-4 aok-align-center"><span class="a-icon-alt">4 out of 5 stars</span></i><span class="a-letter-space"></span><div data-hook="review-badges"><span data-hook="avp-badge" class="a-size-mini a-color-state a-text-bold">Verified Purchase</span></div></div><h4 class="_Y3Itd_single-review-title_2aKRE" data-hook="reviewTitle" lang="en-US">Roborock QRevo 2 Pro - better than narwal and Roomba</h4><div data-hook="review-by-line" class="a-row a-spacing-none"><span data-hook="review-date" class="a-size-small a-color-tertiary">Reviewed in the United States on September 21, 2026</span></div><div data-hook="product-variation-attributes" class="a-row a-spacing-mini"><span data-hook="format-strip-linkless" class="a-size-small a-color-secondary">Colour Name: Black</span></div><div data-hook="reviewTextContainer" class="_Y3Itd_single-review-text-container_325WM"><div data-hook="reviewText" class="a-cardui-deck _Y3Itd_card-deck_37S9P _Y3Itd_mobile_lV1ss" data-a-remove-top-gutter="true" data-a-remove-bottom-gutter="true" name="a-cardui-deck-autoname-29"><div class="a-teaser-describedby-collapsed a-hidden" id="a-cardui-deck-autoname-29-teaser-describedby-collapsed">Brief content visible, double tap to read full content.</div><div class="a-teaser-describedby-expanded a-hidden" id="a-cardui-deck-autoname-29-teaser-describedby-expanded">Full content visible, double tap to read brief content.</div><div data-csa-c-slot-id="customer_review_foreign-R37TQK8F6DR0VA_5_review_text" data-csa-c-type="element" class="a-cardui _Y3Itd_peek-expand_3G9Ub" data-a-card-type="peekExpand" name="a-cardui-deck-autoname-29-card0" data-csa-c-id="b7bc49-krjmpv-kkposo-i8j81c"><div class="a-cardui-body _Y3Itd_no-padding_1A9ha"><div class="a-reactive-container a-reactive-container-transition"><div><div class="a-cardui-content a-cardui-uninitialized" data-a-max-height="100" style="max-height:100px"><div class="_Y3Itd_relative_1SChh"><div data-hook="reviewRichContentContainer" lang="en-US" class="_Y3Itd_contain-rich-content_2IORW"><p><span>I've been through a bunch of these types of robots recently due to various design issues. So, take my review as a matter of perspective versus other robots like it.</span></p><p><span></span></p><p><span>I had originally been avoiding the roborock brand because I had owned two of them before; way back before they adopted the roborock name, they still went by their Chinese name. I got really turned off by them because they had no support for American customers, then as they started rolling out Roborock branded vacuums, they just didn't care about any models prior and stopped supporting me. After having so much problems with other brands I got on the dedicated Reddit thread and found out that roborock has absolutely stepped up its game. They are now the gold standard for reliability and customer support in vacuum + mopping robots. So I'm telling you look no further than the roborock brand but you need to make sure to get the model that suits your needs, don't assume this model is the best, it's what works for me with all tile, plus area rugs.</span></p><p><span></span></p><p><span>I owned two different Narwals:</span></p><p><span>- Freo Z10 Ultra - good but the dock got all gummed up after 1.5 years of use. This is because I use tap water without detergent, so it got scale and mold. I didn't have warranty tried to DIY the fix. It was a nightmare. Wasn't designed modularly, so it gave up after matters got worse. $1k down the drain.</span></p><p><span>- Freo ZT10 Turbo - I gave narwal another try because I chalked it up to my own poor decision to use tap water and no detergent, but it was a disaster. Design issues with the dock and removable washing tray. Always leaking water, had to convince AMZ to allow me to return after ~45 days because Narwal didn't support me, so no more Narwals for me...DONE. The email conversations were SO frustrating with their offers for free accessories that didn't fix the design issue.</span></p><p><span></span></p><p><span>Then I tried Roomba 575 because I thought an "American" brand would support me better. I found issues with the thing right away and never got a response to my support tickets, so returned it within 30 days. The deal breaker was it wouldn't stay connected to WiFi. Cheep, old WiFi hardware wasn't compatible with my modern WiFi router; and trust me I tried some really advanced configuration on my router to dedicate a bare bones 2.5ghz-only SSID to it; no dice. I did some better research after the fact and found out that the iRobot company is going through some serious financial struggles and they're just not worth dealing with anymore. They're basically Chinese owned at this point anyways and can't keep up with competition. I highly advise everyone to stay away from Roomba at this point. Dying brand.</span></p><p><span></span></p><p><span>So onto this robot... what's better:</span></p><p><span>- The Wi-Fi in the app just work. Zero set up issues. The mapping software is easy to use and name all my runes then connect it to Google (or whatever smart home) to get it to clean rooms on demand with just voice. Awesome. Routines and scheduling is easy to set up as well.</span></p><p><span>- The new roller brush design is BADA$$. It really doesn't tangle. I'm actually blown away. It does have both rubber and bristles so that it works fairly well on low pile rugs, but the magic is that the brush is split in two at the middle, which allows the tangling hair to pull towards the middle and get sucked up into the vacuum at the gap. It's brilliant.</span></p><p><span>- it navigates and avoids obstacles like a pro. I'd say my original narwal was about as good at navigation but all the others were terrible compared to this.</span></p><p><span>- The "FlexiArm" works well. My Roomba and second narwal had this as well, all about equal, but I thought it was worth noting no issues so far. It gets in the corners and up against the baseboards.</span></p><p><span>- it's got a really cool feature where it leaves the mop pads behind when you do "carpet first" or vacuum only. This wasn't a huge deal to me, but it avoided an issue I had with other robots where the mop pads would get lodged up under my rugs and pulled off, so I'd have to go fix it...if I was around. Since the mop heads aren't involved at all in vacuuming my rugs, that issue just doesn't happen. Again, brilliant.</span></p><p><span>- battery life is awesome. I have ~1,700 square feet downstairs (of mostly tile) that this thing covers and it has never run out of battery mid-cycle in the middle of a cleaning unless I put it on deep clean for the entire downstairs.</span></p><p><span>- it has a little onboard tank that keeps the mop wet as it's cleaning without going back to the dock. That's much more efficient. The narwals didn't have this, so they were constantly going back to the dock, which may sound like it's going to get things cleaner but it's just inefficient.</span></p><p><span></span></p><p><span>What I don't like, or things that you might want to just be aware of:</span></p><p><span>- no dedicated detergent tank/dispenser. This by biggest one...really the only reason not 5 stars. My other vacuums all had the ability to just snap in a bottle of detergent and it would automatically mix into the cleaning water. Roborock dock doesn't have that feature at all, and they don't give you any with the robot. They sell specialized detergent for these but they don't even mention that using RO or distilled water with detergent will absolutely keep your dock from gumming up like my narwal did. If you don't use ro/ distilled water, you'll get scale, and if you don't use detergent, you'll get mold. So, with this roborock, I will absolutely be manually mixing their specialized detergent into RO water. DO NOT USE ANY OTHER DETERGENT. That is kind of a pain though because you have to measure based on how much you are topping off or let it run dry every time.</span></p><p><span>- Just FYi, what I said earlier about not going back to the dock to re-wet the mops constantly means it is not mopping with hot water most of the time, or at all really. So the advertisement about using 170° water is just about what cleans the mop with at the dock. Not what it is cleaning the floor with. I think that is fine, the mop heads aren't going to stay at 170° very long anyways, but be aware that's what you're getting. This makes it extra important to use the detergent that they barely even acknowledge you should be using.</span></p><p><span>- Roborock app does not allow you to create named zones to reuse. All the other makers did, and this is useful because you can then schedule a zone such as a carpet, with specific vacuuming settings like going over it meticulously multiple times, and make that a routine/automation. This is really a shame because when I put a zone over my living room carpet and tell it to do it meticulously multiple times at maximum strength it actually gets the majority of the dog hair. However, there is no way for me to do this on a scheduled basis unless I tell it to clean my entire living room under such parameters. The rest of my living room doesn't need that kind of meticulousness because it's just tile. If this was a named zone, I could schedule that activity of just my carpet or do a voice command to do it when I notice a little dog hair building up on the carpet. That would be bliss for me, but now I have to do it in the app which is about a 2-minute process each time.</span></p><p><span>- as noted above, when I have this robot go over my living room rug coated in dog hair multiple times meticulously it gets most of the hair but otherwise it does not with just normal "carpet boost". The same "carpet boost" feature on my narwals actually got the majority of the hair. Don't get me wrong, I still manually vacuum my living room rug every 1 or 2 weeks, but I would love it to look better in the meantime without the manual steps I described earlier.</span></p></div></div></div></div><div class="a-reactive-container-gradient"></div></div></div><div data-hook="reviewExpandButtonContainer" class="a-cardui-footer _Y3Itd_no-padding_1A9ha"><div class="a-cardui-expand-control-footer _Y3Itd_read-more-less_2uDYd" data-csa-c-type="widget" data-csa-interaction-events="click" data-csa-c-id="5f16er-27cj2f-7ybt39-5jo7b4"><a href="javascript:void(0)" class="a-cardui-expand-control-footer-button" role="button"><span class="a-expander-icon"><i class="a-css-icon a-css-icon-draw a-css-icon-expand"></i></span><span class="a-see-more a-color-link"><span class="a-see-more-text" style="">Read more</span><span class="a-see-less-text" style="display:none">Read less</span></span></a></div></div></div></div><div data-hook="translationSpinner" class="_Y3Itd_single-review-translation-spinner-container_ypxf2" style="display: none !important;"><div class="a-spinner-wrapper _Y3Itd_single-review-translation-spinner_1tBUJ"><span class="a-spinner a-spinner-medium"></span></div></div></div><div class="a-row"><div class="a-row a-spacing-top-small"><div class="a-row _Y3Itd_single-review-vote-action-bar_10AyR"><div data-hook="reportAbuseButton" data-mix-operations="ReportAbuseOpenDialogClickHandler" data-dialog-kind="sheet" data-asin="B0GXF7ZQPY" data-id="R37TQK8F6DR0VA" data-return-to="DpReview" data-csa-c-type="element" data-csa-c-slot-id="customer_review_foreign-R37TQK8F6DR0VA_5_report_button" data-csa-c-id="czk8e2-92tc4j-eo99lz-o1z7bn"><a aria-label="Report Review" class="a-size-base a-link-normal a-color-secondary a-text-normal" href="javascript:void(0)" role="button">Report</a></div><span data-hook="reportAbuseSendingMessage" class="_Y3Itd_hidden_ySuSy">Sending feedback...</span><div data-hook="reportAbuseSuccess